## What should a local SEO agency actually deliver? {% #local-seo-scope %}

A local SEO agency should improve your Google Business Profile and website around the places and services that generate enquiries. The proposal should identify the work, who implements it and how business outcomes will be measured.

Ask for a written scope across these six areas:

- **Business Profile:** eligibility, ownership, categories, services, opening hours, photos and ongoing corrections.
- **Website:** useful service and location pages, technical fixes, mobile usability and clear booking or enquiry routes.
- **Business information:** accurate contact details on relevant directories and a process for resolving duplicates.
- **Reviews:** a consistent request and response process based on real customer experiences.
- **Local authority:** relevant industry and community relationships that support your business's reputation.
- **Measurement:** location-level visibility, website conversions and qualified enquiries connected to commercial outcomes.

A list of monthly tasks only helps when it addresses a diagnosed problem. Ask why each task matters for your business and what changes you should be able to inspect. My [local SEO service](/services/local-seo/) describes the work I offer if you want direct consultant support.

### Why one map ranking cannot tell the whole story

Google explains local visibility through relevance, distance and prominence. Searcher location affects the result, so an agency cannot make one map position represent an entire town. Accurate profile information and relevant website work help address the factors a business can influence. See [Google's local ranking guidance](https://support.google.com/business/answer/7091?hl=en).

## Which type of provider fits your business? {% #business-model %}

Choose around your business model before comparing agency size. A single shop, a plumber visiting customers and a chain of clinics need different delivery plans even when all three want more local enquiries.

### One customer-facing location

Prioritise the Business Profile, accurate opening hours, the core service pages and the route to call or book. Request a case study from a business with similar competition and customer behaviour. A local campaign for a restaurant is not automatically evidence of expertise with a dental practice.

### A business that visits customers

Ask how the provider will represent the real service area and prioritise the jobs you want. A town page should explain actual availability, services and relevant evidence. Changing a place name across dozens of otherwise identical pages adds little for a customer deciding whom to hire.

### Several premises or branches

Ask for a location inventory, clear profile permissions and a plan for opening hours, reviews and pages at each branch. Reporting should reveal weak locations individually. An improving total can conceal a branch losing enquiries.

Confirm who supplies branch information and who approves changes. Central governance and local staff input both need an owner.

Use the filter above to form a shortlist. The fit labels reflect published capabilities; ask each provider to confirm experience with your exact model.

## How much does local SEO cost in the UK? {% #local-seo-cost %}

Compare the full scope and contract cost rather than a headline monthly fee. Setup work, the number of locations, website implementation and reporting all change what a local SEO engagement includes.

My [published local SEO service](/services/local-seo/) currently starts at **£600 per month**. One-off setup projects start at **£800**. These are starting prices for my service, not a market average or a like-for-like quote for the providers listed here. Confirm the agreed work and VAT treatment in writing.

Each profile states whether a provider publishes a starting price or requires a quote. Public package prices can have different deliverables and billing terms. A low entry price does not establish the cost of the work your site needs.

### What belongs in a comparable quote?

Request the setup fee, monthly fee, minimum term, notice period and total initial commitment. Add the cost of development, content, photography, reporting tools and any advertising that sits outside the quoted service.

**Illustrative calculation:** a £750 monthly fee over six months totals £4,500. A £500 setup fee and £1,000 of additional website work bring the commitment to **£6,000 before any applicable VAT**. These are example numbers, not a supplier quote.

Send the same brief to each shortlisted provider. Ask them to price the same locations and outcomes. The downloadable template on this page gives you a starting point.

## How do you tell whether local SEO is working? {% #measure-results %}

Measure qualified enquiries and business outcomes alongside visibility. A map ranking, a profile interaction and a paying customer are different events. Your report should show the connection without treating them as interchangeable.

### Check the definitions behind the dashboard

Google's Business Profile **Calls** metric counts clicks on the call button. It does not prove that a call connected or became a qualified lead. Direction requests do not prove store visits. Google also says profile performance can include organic and advertising activity. Read [Google's performance metric definitions](https://support.google.com/business/answer/9918094?hl=en).

Ask the provider to explain each number before agreeing targets:

- **Local visibility:** track a consistent set of services and search locations. Keep the same grid, settings and dates when comparing map reports.
- **Website demand:** inspect relevant search queries and landing pages. Separate branded searches from people discovering the business through a service query.
- **Enquiries:** record calls, forms or bookings. Classify spam, existing customers and enquiries outside the service area.
- **Commercial results:** record qualified leads, appointments and won work where the business can attribute them reliably.

### Inspect a case study before hiring

Request the starting position, dates, location count and actual work delivered. Check whether the reported gains came from SEO, advertising, a new website or a combination. Ask for the limits of the measurement and a reference from a comparable business.

A screenshot without a date range or baseline is incomplete evidence. A percentage increase from a small starting number can also be misleading without the underlying counts.

## What should happen in the first 90 days? {% #first-90-days %}

Use the first 90 days to judge diagnosis, implementation and measurement. Agree deliverables and review points before the work starts. Treat this as an example delivery framework rather than a promise that every business will rank within three months.

1. **Days 1-30: establish the baseline.** Confirm ownership and eligibility. Audit the profile, website and business information. Agree priority services, locations and lead definitions. Record what will be changed and who can approve it.
2. **Days 31-60: implement the priorities.** Correct important profile and website issues. Improve the pages closest to enquiries. Put a consistent review process in place. Check that forms, calls and booking routes work on mobile.
3. **Days 61-90: evaluate and adapt.** Compare equivalent reporting periods. Review qualified enquiries, the changes made and remaining constraints. Decide whether the next priority is coverage, conversion, technical work or a different service area.

Complex websites and several locations can need a longer implementation phase. A provider should explain that dependency early. Ask for a visible change log so you can distinguish work completed from recommendations still waiting on your team.

## Which Google Business Profile practices should you reject? {% #profile-rules %}

Reject tactics that misrepresent the business or manufacture reviews. Require your provider to explain how its proposed profile setup and review process follow Google's current rules. A short-term visibility claim does not justify putting an important customer channel at risk.

### Real premises and service areas

Use the real business name and eligible locations. A rented mailing address alone does not make a location eligible. A business that travels to customers should use the appropriate service-area setup and hide a non-customer-facing address. Additional premises have their own eligibility requirements. Check [Google's business representation guidelines](https://support.google.com/business/answer/3038177?hl=en).

Ask how a provider handles duplicates and verification problems before allowing bulk changes. A suspension needs diagnosis against the specific reason; creating replacement profiles without resolving the problem is not a reliable recovery plan.

### Honest review requests

Ask customers for reviews based on their actual experience. Do not buy reviews, offer discounts for them or send only happy customers to Google while diverting unhappy customers elsewhere. Google prohibits incentives and selectively soliciting positive reviews. See [Google's review policy](https://support.google.com/contributionpolicy/answer/7400114?hl=en).

### Account ownership and exit terms

Keep control of the business accounts and grant the access needed for the work. Confirm who owns content, tracking numbers, reporting data and deliverables. Require a handover plan and a clear process for removing access when the engagement ends.

Ask what any ranking guarantee actually covers. A contractual promise to continue working is not control over Google's results or a guarantee of enquiries.

## Do you need an agency nearby, a consultant or a DIY start? {% #agency-or-consultant %}

Hire for the delivery you need and the resources available. A nearby office is useful for meetings or on-site work. It does not establish that the provider understands your local market or can deliver the proposed improvements.

Choose a consultant when you want direct specialist involvement and a focused scope. Choose an agency when several disciplines or locations need coordinated delivery. Ask to meet the people assigned to your work in either model. The [broader UK SEO company comparison](/blog/best-seo-companies-uk/) covers options beyond local search.

A DIY start can be sensible for one straightforward location. Check your business information, update opening hours, add useful photos and make it easy to contact the business. Escalate when you lack time, need technical implementation or face a profile issue that you cannot diagnose.

Avoid buying software and a retainer that duplicate the same tasks. A citation platform or rank tracker can support the work, but it does not decide your strategy or implement website changes by itself.

## How was this shortlist selected? {% #methodology %}

This is an editorial shortlist based on public local SEO service pages and relevant work checked on **12 September 2026**. It is not an exhaustive market survey or a scored ranking. Providers appear alphabetically in the comparison and profiles.

The selection prioritises an explicit local-search offer, inspectable services or work, a clear delivery model and a useful fit for UK businesses. Filter labels describe my reading of the published offer. They do not certify specialist expertise or limit what a provider can do.

Public case studies are the providers' own claims. I have not independently audited their analytics, hired every company or obtained comparable quotes. Published prices are identified separately from quote-only services. Confirm current fees, capacity and scope directly.

**Commercial disclosure:** I sell local SEO services. Sunny Patel is included in the main list with a visible author label. Apply the same evidence checks to my business. Tom Riley has also included me in his GEO and AI SEO consultant guides; that connection is disclosed in his profile. Those mentions are practitioner roundups rather than independent audits of results.
